# Every secret this service needs, in one file.
#
# Copy this to `secrets.env`, fill it in, and keep that copy in your password
# vault. `secrets.env` is gitignored by the `*.env` rule; this example is
# tracked and is the documentation. After cloning on a new machine, dropping
# one file in place is the whole of the setup.
#
# It has two destinations, and keeping them apart is the point:
#
#   make secrets-check     parse this file, name every missing or empty key,
#                          print nothing else. Never echoes a value.
#   make secrets-push      the DEPLOY group -> GitHub repository secrets, so CD
#                          can log in and restart a binary.
#   make secrets-install   the RUNTIME group -> config.toml on the server, over
#                          SSH, mode 600.
#
# Runtime secrets deliberately never reach GitHub. Actions needs exactly enough
# to ship a binary; it has no business holding a Stripe live key. If CD is ever
# compromised, the blast radius should stop at "can deploy code", which is bad
# enough without also being "can charge cards".
#
# Generate the two keys we invent ourselves with:
#   openssl rand -base64 48


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DEPLOY GROUP -> GitHub repository secrets, via `make secrets-push`
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The Hetzner konsoleH shell account, left blank on purpose.
#
# This repository is public. The hostname is resolvable in DNS anyway, but the
# account name and the non-standard port are not, and together they are most of
# what narrows a brute-force attempt -- so there is no reason for a public
# repository to name the machine that runs the thing. Find them in konsoleH
# under Access > SSH, or in your vault copy of this file.
DEPLOY_SSH_HOST=
DEPLOY_SSH_USER=
DEPLOY_SSH_PORT=

# The deploy key, base64-encoded so this stays a single-line-per-key file that
# a password vault can hold without mangling newlines.
#
#   ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -f ~/.ssh/dropin-forms-deploy -C dropin-forms-cd -N ''
#   base64 -w0 < ~/.ssh/dropin-forms-deploy
#
# Then append the .pub to ~/.ssh/authorized_keys on the server.
#
# Be clear-eyed about this one: on konsoleH there is no root and therefore no
# sudoers entry to narrow the key to a single installer command, the way the
# VPS sibling does. Whoever holds this key has a shell on the account. That is
# accepted deliberately. It was also the main argument for CD running on a tag
# rather than on every push -- which it no longer does: the merge is the
# decision, and the `production` environment is where a reviewer goes if one is
# wanted. The key's blast radius is unchanged either way, so what actually
# limits it is that this file never reaches GitHub: the Stripe keys, the SMTP
# password and the grant signing key go to the server from a laptop, so a
# compromise of the Actions secrets stops at "can deploy a binary".
DEPLOY_SSH_KEY_B64=

# Pinned host key, so CD never needs StrictHostKeyChecking=no. Base64 for the
# same reason as above.
#
#   ssh-keyscan -p <port> -t ed25519 <host> | base64 -w0
DEPLOY_KNOWN_HOSTS_B64=


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RUNTIME GROUP -> config.toml on the server, via `make secrets-install`
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Stripe. The publishable key is not secret but lives here so that rotating the
# pair is one edit in one place. The webhook secret comes from the endpoint you
# create in the Stripe dashboard, not from the API keys page -- they are
# different things and mixing them up costs an afternoon.
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=
STRIPE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# Both of the keys above stand or fall together. `secrets render` refuses a
# partly filled pair rather than writing a config the binary will not start on
# -- a key with no webhook secret means money collected and no order ever
# marked paid, with nothing anywhere saying so.
#
# There is no port or docroot to set. The webhook shares the embed listener at
# https://f.schoenstatt.link/stripe/webhook, mounted outside that surface's
# origin gate, so it needs no subdomain and no certificate of its own. That URL
# is what goes into the Stripe dashboard.

# THE WEBHOOK SECRET IS PER ENDPOINT, AND PER MODE.
#
# A sandbox endpoint's secret will not verify a live event. Both begin whsec_,
# so nothing here or in the binary can tell them apart: a live API key paired
# with the sandbox webhook secret starts cleanly, charges real cards, and marks
# nothing paid. You would find that out by comparing a bank statement against a
# list of pending orders. So when you switch to live keys, create the endpoint
# in live mode and take that endpoint's secret.
#
# FOUR EVENTS go on that endpoint, and they are the four this binary acts on. Anything else Stripe
# sends is acknowledged and ignored, so attaching more is harmless noise and
# attaching fewer is silent: the order simply never leaves "pending", and
# nothing anywhere says why.
#
#   checkout.session.completed
#       The one that matters. Marks the submission paid -- but only when the
#       session's payment_status is paid or no_payment_required. A completed
#       session is not always a paid one: a delayed payment method completes
#       the session and settles afterwards, and treating that as paid would put
#       a lunch on the list nobody has paid for.
#
#   checkout.session.async_payment_failed
#       The delayed counterpart of the above, for a payment method that settles
#       after the session does. Attach it even though the feast form takes
#       cards, which settle immediately: a form that takes cards today may take
#       a bank debit tomorrow, and without this that failure is invisible --
#       the completed event having deliberately said nothing.
#
#   payment_intent.payment_failed
#       Marks the submission failed, so a card that was declined shows as
#       declined rather than as an order still waiting to be paid.
#
#   charge.dispute.created
#       Somebody has disputed a charge with their bank. Recorded rather than
#       acted on, because the money has already left and the answer is a person
#       looking at it, not a status change. It is here so the office finds out
#       from the app rather than from a bank letter.
#
# Confirming the secret is right, without a real charge: from the endpoint's
# page in the Stripe dashboard, send a test event. A 200 in its attempt log
# means the signature verified. A 400 ("that notification is not signed by
# Stripe") means the secret in this file is not this endpoint's.


# KEEP THE SANDBOX KEYS TOO. They are optional, and they are how you rehearse.
#
# These three are not read by the service and are not sent anywhere by
# default. `secrets render` and `secrets push` write only the settings they
# name, one at a time, so a spare key here is inert -- which is worth knowing
# rather than assuming, because loadConfig refuses a config carrying a setting
# it does not understand, and a renderer that copied every line would turn a
# spare key into a service that will not start. scripts/secrets-render-test.sh
# asserts that, so it stays true.
#
# What they are for: once the keys above are live, a rehearsal on the real page
# would charge a real card. So `install` takes a mode.
#
#     make secrets-install-test     config.toml with the keys below
#     deploy/deploy.sh restart      the startup line says: payments test mode
#
#     ... rehearse on the real page with 4242 4242 4242 4242 ...
#
#     make secrets-install          config.toml with the live keys again
#     deploy/deploy.sh restart      the line says: payments LIVE
#
# Nothing else in the rendered config differs between the two -- same ports,
# same origins, same grant key, same database -- which is the point: a
# rehearsal against a different configuration is a rehearsal of something
# else. That too is asserted by the test above.
#
# Both modes need their OWN webhook endpoint in Stripe, because an endpoint
# belongs to one mode. Create the same URL twice, once in the sandbox and once
# live, attach the same four events to each, and keep each one's whsec_ in its
# own slot here. Then the pair always moves together and there is no way to end
# up with a live key checking sandbox signatures.
#
# `render test` refuses a STRIPE_TEST_SECRET_KEY that does not begin sk_test_.
# That slot has exactly one meaning, and a live key pasted into it would charge
# a real card during a rehearsal. The other direction is deliberately not
# checked: the keys above are whatever this installation runs on, and they were
# sandbox keys until the day it went live.
STRIPE_TEST_SECRET_KEY=
STRIPE_TEST_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=
STRIPE_TEST_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# The publishable keys in both pairs are unused today. Nothing renders them
# into config.toml, because Track A never loads Stripe.js -- the card is
# entered on Stripe's own page. They are here so that rotating a pair is one
# edit in one place, and because the in-page Payment Element will need them.

# Mail. The mailbox is at the zone apex on purpose: Exim on this host rejects
# mail for subdomains of schoenstatt.link, so neither f. nor forms. can send as
# itself. SPF and DKIM already pass for the apex, which matters because a
# magic link in a spam folder means nobody can sign in.
# Blank for the same reason as above, plus one of its own: an address written
# out in a public repository is an address that gets scraped.
SMTP_HOST=
SMTP_PORT=587
SMTP_USER=
SMTP_PASSWORD=
MAIL_FROM=

# Where a new submission is announced, in addition to a form's own `notify`
# list and every account holding the `results` role on it.
#
# It becomes mail.notify in config.toml. Leaving it empty is allowed and means
# the other two are the only ways anybody hears; a submission that reaches
# nobody by any of the three is logged as a warning rather than passing
# silently.
ADMIN_NOTIFY_EMAIL=

# Signs the submission grant: the HMAC over form id, form version, nonce and
# expiry that pins server-chosen state into a public POST. Rotating it
# invalidates every form page currently open in somebody's browser, which is
# survivable -- they get a fresh grant on reload -- but do not do it while
# tickets are selling.
#
# Required, not optional: a form page cannot be rendered without a grant to
# put in it, so the service refuses to start without this. At least 32
# characters. Generate one with:
#
#   openssl rand -base64 48
GRANT_HMAC_KEY=

# A one-time URL that mints the first session without email, so a mail
# misconfiguration cannot lock you out of your own app. Consumed on first use
# and then inert. Set it, sign in, create your account, and it stops mattering.
BOOTSTRAP_SIGNIN_SECRET=


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NOT SECRET, but it lives here so one file is the whole configuration
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Everything below this line is also pushed to GitHub as an Actions *variable*
# by `make secrets-push`, because CD needs it and none of it is worth hiding.
#
# Which means it can drift: change a port here, forget to push, and a deploy
# from a laptop keeps working while the next pushed one refuses. That happened.
# `make secrets-check` now compares the two and names any difference, so run it
# after editing anything in this section.

# Two hostnames, two document roots, two loopback ports. The surface a request
# reached is decided by which socket accepted it, because ProxyPreserveHost
# cannot be set in .htaccess and the proxied Host header is therefore
# 127.0.0.1:<port> for both. See docs/design/drop-in-forms.md section 3.1.
#
# These are not ours to assume: the host is shared, and 8410 turned out to be
# held by another account. `deploy/deploy.sh ports` lists what is free.
EMBED_HOST=f.schoenstatt.link
ADMIN_HOST=forms.schoenstatt.link
EMBED_PORT=8412
ADMIN_PORT=8413

# Who may frame an embedded form, space separated, until each form carries its
# own list. This becomes the CSP frame-ancestors directive, so an empty value
# means nothing may frame anything -- the policy fails closed on purpose. A
# list rather than one value because frame-ancestors checks *every* ancestor,
# not just the immediate parent, and Squarespace nests pages inside editor
# chrome.
EMBED_ALLOWED_ORIGINS="https://schoenstatt-austin.us"

# How many database backups to keep on the server. They are taken on every
# deploy, while the writer is stopped.
KEEP_BACKUPS=14

# Paths are relative to the remote home. Do not write a leading `~` here: an
# unquoted assignment is tilde-expanded by your *local* shell, and the server
# is then sent an absolute path under your own home directory that means
# nothing there.
APP_DIR=dropin-forms
EMBED_DOCROOT=public_html/f.schoenstatt.link/public
ADMIN_DOCROOT=public_html/forms.schoenstatt.link/public

# Everything in this last section except the origins list is pushed to GitHub
# as a repository *variable* rather than a secret, by `make secrets-push`. A
# variable is readable in a workflow log, which is right for a port number and
# a path and would be wrong for a key.
